rangements", "Neoplastic aetiology: considered but ranked lower due to lack of clear tumor-related symptoms"], "alarm_features": ["weight loss of 10 pounds over the past month", "night sweats", "leukocyte count: 57,500/mm^3 with 35% blasts"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Leukemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5686871050783991, "posterior": 0.6114152845230459, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count supports the diagnosis of Acute Leukemia. The combination of weight loss, night sweats, and leukocytosis does not strongly support Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm. The patient's presentation of malaise, weakness, and night sweats provides some evidence for Lymphoproliferative Disorder, but the absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly argues against it. The subacute onset of symptoms and the presence of night sweats do not argue against Acute Leukemia, and the presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count argues against Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["What are the patient's symptoms?", "What are the laboratory results?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Bone marrow biopsy", "Cytogenetic analysis"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.005727114450655182, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count supports the diagnosis of Acute Leukemia, specifically Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) and other forms of acute leukemia. The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells could support the diagnosis of Biphenotypic Acute Leukemia. The absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly does not strongly argue against the diagnosis of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells does not argue against the diagnosis of Acute Myelogenous Leukemia (AML). The absence of specific immunophenotypic markers does not argue against the diagnosis of Acute Undifferentiated Leukemia."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoproliferative Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.04212497074654808, "posterior": 0.020617612022358654, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count supports the diagnosis of Acute Leukemia. The combination of weight loss, night sweats, and leukocytosis does not strongly support Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm. The patient's presentation of malaise, weakness, and night sweats provides some evidence for Lymphoproliferative Disorder, but the absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly argues against it. The subacute onset of symptoms and the presence of night sweats do not argue against Acute Leukemia, and the presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count argues against Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm.", "The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count, along with symptoms of malaise, weakness, and night sweats, supports the diagnosis of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) and other forms of acute leukemia. The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells could support the diagnosis of Biphenotypic Acute Leukemia, while the absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly may argue against certain diagnoses but does not conclusively rule them out. The symptoms also provide some evidence for Lymphoproliferative Disorder, though the absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly argues against it. The analysis does not provide conclusive evidence against Acute Myelogenous Leukemia (AML) or Acute Undifferentiated Leukemia based on the absence of specific immunophenotypic markers or the presence of myeloid and lymphoid blast cells."], "evidence_against": ["The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count supports the diagnosis of Acute Leukemia, specifically Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) and other forms of acute leukemia. The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells could support the diagnosis of Biphenotypic Acute Leukemia. The absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly does not strongly argue against the diagnosis of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells does not argue against the diagnosis of Acute Myelogenous Leukemia (AML). The absence of specific immunophenotypic markers does not argue against the diagnosis of Acute Undifferentiated Leukemia."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["What are the patient's symptoms?", "What is the patient's medical history?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Flow cytometry", "Bone marrow biopsy"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.2, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.014317786126637954, "danger": 0.1, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The presence of 35% blasts in the leukocyte count supports the diagnosis of Acute Leukemia, specifically Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) and other forms of acute leukemia. The presence of both myeloid and lymphoid blast cells could support the diagnosis of Biphenotypic Acute Leukemia. The absence of specific lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly does not strongly argue against 